# Project Overrides

This file is populated during the project inception phase. When the agent and user agree that a framework default doesn't fit this specific project, the override is recorded here so all future conversations respect it.

**If this file is empty, all framework defaults from rules 02-06 apply as written.**

---

## How to read this file

Each override follows this format:

```
### [Rule ID] — [Short description of what's being overridden]
**Default:** What the framework rule prescribes.
**Override:** What this project does instead.
**Rationale:** Why the deviation makes sense for this project.
```

---

## Active Overrides

### 03 — Domain pattern
**Default:** Transactions as source of truth with derived state (ledger model).
**Override:** Simple CRUD with direct entity storage. Expenses are stored and queried directly — no ledger, no derived holdings.
**Rationale:** Tally is a capture + tag + export tool. There is no portfolio, no positions, no history-dependent state. Expenses are independent records. Monthly totals are derived via simple aggregation, not event sourcing.

### 03 — Navigation pattern
**Default:** Tab + Sheet navigation with 4-5 top-level tabs.
**Override:** Single-screen home with FAB + sheets for actions. No tab bar. No web sidebar (web deprioritised).
**Rationale:** Tally has one primary screen (expense list) with modal flows for creation, editing, export, and settings. A tab bar would create empty destinations. The home screen IS the app.

### 04 — Color scheme
**Default:** Dark mode first.
**Override:** Light mode first. Warm neutral background (#F6F5F3) with muted forest green accents (#346049). No gradients, no shadows, no glossy effects. Flat only.
**Rationale:** The brand direction is "focused, calm, confident, disciplined, modern, efficient." The warm neutral canvas with restrained green highlights creates a premium tool aesthetic. Dark mode can be added later as an option.

### 04 — Typography
**Default:** Generic font factory approach.
**Override:** SF Pro for UI, SF Mono for amounts. Native iOS font stack only (web deprioritised).
**Rationale:** Tally is iOS-native. Using system fonts ensures crisp rendering and visual consistency with iOS.

### 03 — Architecture pattern
**Default:** Purely local, single-platform app architecture.
**Override:** iOS-only with iCloud sync. SwiftUI + SwiftData + CloudKit. No proprietary backend, no server-side user data storage, no custom auth. User data lives entirely in the user's iCloud. Attachments stored as CKAsset. Auth is implicit via Apple ID / iCloud. Web app deprioritised (Phase 3 at earliest, via CloudKit JS if demand warrants).
**Rationale:** iCloud-only dramatically reduces liability (no data custodian obligations), compliance burden (no GDPR for user content), server cost (zero), and security complexity. It also increases perceived privacy. Tally does not need a proprietary backend — the value is in the client experience, not in server infrastructure.

### 05 — Monetisation model
**Default:** Freemium + ads with ad-gated actions and daily caps.
**Override:** Freemium with banner ads only. Free tier: unlimited expenses, unlimited receipt uploads, unlimited tags, unlimited viewing, 1 CSV export per calendar month, iCloud sync. Pro ($4.99/mo): unlimited CSV, PDF export, monthly auto-export, no ads, OCR auto-fill, advanced filters. No interstitial ads. No rewarded ads. No inline sponsored rows. No upload caps. No storage caps (user-owned iCloud). Manual entry and unlimited tags are NEVER gated. See `MONETISATION_BRIEF.md` for full rules.
**Rationale:** Tally's free tier must feel fully usable for daily expense logging. The only friction point is export volume (1 CSV/month). Pro adds time-saving automation (OCR, auto-export, advanced filters), additional export formats (PDF), and a cleaner experience (no ads). No artificial pain. Storage is user-owned via iCloud so gating it would be dishonest.

### 04 — Gradient and shadow usage
**Default:** Brand gradient used for hero accents, primary CTAs, active states.
**Override:** No gradients. No drop shadows. No glossy effects. Flat, solid colors only. Green is used sparingly as a highlight — primary actions, icons, active states only. Never for large backgrounds.
**Rationale:** The brand direction explicitly calls for flat, engineered aesthetic. No decorative UI. Gradients, shadows, and gloss conflict with the disciplined, minimal identity.
